WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.913
A lift has a weight limit. Inside it, safe. Outside, not. An expansion with a negative or fractional power also has a limit, on x. It is valid when it gives the true value of the bracket.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:33.368
Take one plus x plus x squared and so on. At x equal to a half, the sums are one point five, one point seven five, one point eight seven five. They settle near two. At x equal to two, the sums are three, seven, fifteen. They never settle.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:47.209
The rule: one plus X, to a power that is not a positive whole number, is valid when modulus X is less than one. The modulus is the size, ignoring the sign. It means X is between minus one and one.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:58.550
In one plus b x, X is b x. So modulus b x is less than one. Divide by modulus b: modulus x is less than one over modulus b.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:05.758
So the question says: write down, state, or find the range of x for which your expansion is valid.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:14.976
Step one: look at the bracket, one plus b x. Step two: write modulus b x less than one.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:23.863
Step three: divide by modulus b. State modulus x less than c, or minus c less than x less than c.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:32.011
The trap: a bracket that does not start with one. Three plus x must first become three, times one plus x over three.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:47.824
June twenty twenty three. From part a, the expansion is of one plus x over three, to the power minus three. Write down the range of values of x for which the expansion is valid. One mark.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:58.307
Step one: the bracket. It is one plus x over three, so X is x over three. The bracket starts with one, so no constant needs taking out.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:11.806
Step two: modulus x over three is less than one. Multiply both sides by three. Modulus x is less than three. That means x is between minus three and three.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:25.920
A tempting wrong route gives modulus x less than one third. Wrong. One over one third is three. Check: at x equal to two, X is two thirds, less than one, so two is allowed.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:37.925
Test a value outside. At x equal to four, the terms are one, minus four, ten point six seven, minus twenty three point seven. They grow, so four is outside.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:46.168
So the expansion is valid for modulus x less than three. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:58.325
A common mistake is writing x less than three. That allows x equal to minus ten. Valid x go from minus three to three.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:15.471
Now you try, from June twenty twenty four. From part a, the expansion is of two over the square root of one plus three x. State the range of x for which it is valid. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:28.885
Rewrite as a power: two, times one plus three x, to the minus a half. The two sits outside the bracket, so it does not change the range. X is three x.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:41.719
Now modulus three x is less than one. Divide by three. Modulus x is less than one third. So x is between minus one third and one third.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:52.543
The common wrong answer is modulus x less than three. That multiplies instead of dividing. Test x equal to one: X is three, so the terms grow.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:59.629
Remember: look at the bracket, write modulus X less than one, divide by b, then state the range of x.
